MAJOR FINDING -- one job board is not the job market
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
[2026-09-03 03:35] source_added       Searched ZipRecruiter with the SAME queries that returned zero
                                      results on Indeed. ZipRecruiter returned 34 EEG matches within
                                      40 miles of Hollywood and 12 neurodiagnostic matches, including:
                                        - Cleveland Clinic "EEG Technician Apprentice", Weston FL,
                                          $52.5k-$80k, 9 days old  -> scored 88, APPLY NOW
                                        - Memorial Healthcare "EEG Technician", Joe DiMaggio
                                          Children's Hospital, Hollywood FL (her own city), $50.5k-$77k
                                        - Baptist Health "Neurophysiology Tech Assistant", Miami
                                        - Baptist Health "Pool EEG Technician", Boca Raton
                                      Earlier this log recorded "there are currently no EEG or
                                      neurodiagnostic trainee postings in South Florida." That
                                      conclusion was WRONG. It was true of Indeed and I generalised
                                      it to the market.
                                      PROCESS FIX: never conclude a category is empty from a single
                                      source. Always cross-check at least two boards before reporting
                                      a gap. 9 listings added; the board's top slot changed from a
                                      62-point sleep-tech contract to an 88-point named apprenticeship.

================================================================================
REFLECTION -- 2026-09-03
================================================================================
WHAT WORKED
  - Asking Stacey four questions up front instead of guessing. "EEG apprenticeships,
    path to psychiatry" is not something I would have inferred from the CV alone.
  - Keeping all judgement in preferences.json and none in board.py. Two of the four
    bugs were fixed by editing data, with no code change and no risk.
  - Writing the failing test first, every time. It proved each bug was real and
    proved each fix worked, which is what debug.log is supposed to adjudicate.
  - Reading the CV properly. Advanced Haitian Creole in Broward healthcare is a real
    hiring edge and it is now a scored component, not a footnote.

WHAT DID NOT WORK
  - I let my own analyst notes into the scoring text. The board spent an hour
    confidently recommending a job on evidence it had invented. This is the single
    worst failure mode for this system and it shipped in the first version.
  - I declared the EEG market empty after searching one source. Wrong, and it would
    have cost Stacey the Cleveland Clinic apprenticeship.
  - I burned real time retrying a rate-limited API instead of switching sources sooner.

CHANGES TO MY OWN PROCESS, FOR NEXT SESSION
  1. Evidence and commentary never share a field. If a value is used for scoring,
     nothing subjective goes in it. Assume this bug will try to come back.
  2. Two sources minimum before reporting that a category has no listings.
  3. When an API rate-limits twice, switch sources or ship with the gap flagged.
     Do not keep retrying.
  4. Thin descriptions are the main thing still degrading accuracy. Next session:
     pull full posting text for the top 5 before anything else. Training signals
     live in the body, and that is the single heaviest component after role fit.
  5. Concrete next action for Stacey that is NOT a job application: get the BLS/CPR
     card. About $60-90, one day, and it removes a screening filter from a large
     share of these postings.

